<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cai Yong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">133–192</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cai Yong was a scholar, calligrapher, musician and official of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. He supervised the engraving of the Xiping Stone Classics, the official texts of the Confucian classics cut in stone at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 175–183. Out of favour at court, he later served the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a> and died in prison after Dong's assassination in 192. His daughter Cai Wenji was a celebrated poet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cai-yong.jpg" alt="Cai Yong" width="320" height="507" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wang Qi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cai_yong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
